    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Evening Shade developed as a small rural community in Sequoyah County during the early twentieth century following Oklahoma's admission to the Union.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/evening_shade_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Evening Shade was previously part of the Sequoyah District of the Cherokee Nation established in 1851.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/evening_shade_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Evening Shade Community Center evolved from a historic one-room schoolhouse and church into a hub for senior meals and family events.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/evening_shade_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Evening Shade maintains a quiet, agrarian character influenced by its Cherokee heritage and proximity to the Arkansas River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/evening_shade_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Agriculture in Evening Shade diversified from a dependency on cotton to include soybeans, wheat, and cattle ranching.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/evening_shade_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Evening Shade spans over thirteen square miles of land and water, consisting primarily of small streams and ponds.",
